---
/**
 * /agents/: the MCP server for AI assistants (docs/design/plan-format.md, section 7): what it
 * does, how to add it to each client, example prompts, privacy and limits.
 */
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
import { MCP_PATH, MCP_PRIVACY, SITE_NAME } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'Room Planner for AI Assistants: Free MCP Server for Claude, ChatGPT & More';
const description =
  'Connect Claude, ChatGPT, VS Code, Cursor or Claude Code to Room Planner: your AI assistant lays out a room, checks it for clashes and blocked doors, and gives you a link to edit it.';

const site = Astro.site ?? new URL(Astro.url.origin);
const abs = (path: string) => new URL(withBase(path), site).toString();
const endpoint = abs(MCP_PATH);

const vscode = JSON.stringify(
  { servers: { 'room-planner': { type: 'http', url: endpoint } } },
  null,
  2,
);
const cursor = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { 'room-planner': { url: endpoint } } }, null, 2);

const tools = [
  {
    name: 'create_plan',
    text: 'turns the rooms and furniture the assistant describes into a checked plan and a link that opens it in the planner. In Claude, ChatGPT and other apps that support MCP Apps, the drawing appears in the chat with any problems marked.',
  },
  {
    name: 'check_layout',
    text: 'lists overlapping pieces, pieces outside the room, furniture in a door’s swing or doorway, and gaps tighter than a walkway, so the assistant can fix them before it shows you anything.',
  },
  {
    name: 'read_plan',
    text: 'reads a Room Planner link you paste into the chat, so the assistant can change your existing plan.',
  },
];

const prompts = [
  'My bedroom is 11 × 12 ft. The door is on the bottom wall near the left corner and there is a window in the middle of the top wall. Fit a queen bed, two nightstands, a dresser and a desk, and give me a Room Planner link.',
  'Lay out a 350 × 400 cm living room with a three-seat sofa facing a TV stand, a coffee table and two armchairs. Keep 90 cm walkways and nothing in front of the door.',
  'Here is my plan: (paste your Room Planner link). Move the desk under the window and check that nothing blocks the door.',
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': `${abs('agents/')}#page`,
    name: 'Room Planner for AI assistants',
    description,
    url: abs('agents/'),
    inLanguage: 'en',
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${abs('')}#website` },
    about: { '@id': `${abs('')}#app` },
  },
];

const code = 'block bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-lg p-3 text-sm overflow-x-auto';
---

<Content
  title={title}
  description={description}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'For AI assistants', path: 'agents/' }]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Use {SITE_NAME} from an AI assistant</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 mb-8">
    {SITE_NAME} has a free MCP server. Connect it to Claude, ChatGPT or your code editor and ask the
    assistant to lay out a room: it writes the plan to scale, checks it for clashes and blocked
    doors, and gives you a link that opens the plan in the planner, where you can drag, rotate,
    measure, export and share it. No account and no API key.
  </p>

  <article class="space-y-10 [&_h2]:text-xl [&_h2]:font-bold [&_h2]:text-slate-900 [&_h2]:mb-3 [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:text-slate-800 [&_h3]:mt-5 [&_h3]:mb-1 [&_p]:mb-3 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5 [&_ul]:space-y-1 [&_ul]:mb-3 [&_ol]:list-decimal [&_ol]:pl-5 [&_ol]:space-y-1 [&_ol]:mb-3">
    <section id="url">
      <h2>The server URL</h2>
      <code class={code} tabindex="0">
        {endpoint}
      </code>
      <p class="mt-3">
        It is a remote MCP server over Streamable HTTP, with no sign-in (choose “no authentication”
        or “none” if your app asks).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="setup">
      <h2>Add it to your assistant</h2>

      <h3>Claude (claude.ai and the desktop app)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open <strong>Settings → Connectors</strong> and choose{' '}
          <strong>Add custom connector</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>Name it “Room Planner”, paste the server URL above, and add it.</li>
        <li>In a chat, turn Room Planner on from the tools menu, then ask for a layout.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>ChatGPT</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open <strong>Settings → Apps &amp; Connectors → Advanced settings</strong> and turn on{' '}
          <strong>Developer mode</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Back in <strong>Apps &amp; Connectors</strong>, choose <strong>Create</strong>, paste the
          server URL, set authentication to <strong>No authentication</strong>, and create it.
        </li>
        <li>In a chat, pick Room Planner from the tools (+) menu, then ask for a layout.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Claude Code</h3>
      <code class={code} tabindex="0">
        claude mcp add --transport http room-planner {endpoint}
      </code>

      <h3>VS Code (GitHub Copilot agent mode)</h3>
      <p>
        Run <strong>MCP: Add Server</strong> from the Command Palette, choose HTTP and paste the
        URL, or add this to <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>:
      </p>
      <pre class={code} tabindex="0">{vscode}</pre>

      <h3>Cursor</h3>
      <p>
        Add this to <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> (or <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> in a project):
      </p>
      <pre class={code} tabindex="0">{cursor}</pre>

      <h3>Find it in the MCP Registry</h3>
      <p>
        Room Planner is listed in the official{' '}
        <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io" class="text-indigo-700 underline">
          MCP Registry
        </a>{' '}
        as <code>io.github.EricAndrechek/room-planner</code>, so apps that browse the registry can
        add it by name.
      </p>

      <h3>Other apps</h3>
      <p>
        Any MCP client that supports remote servers over Streamable HTTP can use the URL as it is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="tools">
      <h2>What the assistant can do</h2>
      <ul>
        {tools.map((t) => (
          <li>
            <code>{t.name}</code> {t.text}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Lengths can be in feet, inches, meters or centimeters: the assistant uses the units you use.
        For now a link opens one rectangular room with its doors, windows and rectangular furniture,
        which is what the planner edits today.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="examples">
      <h2>Things to ask</h2>
      <ul>
        {prompts.map((p) => (
          <li>“{p}”</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy">
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>{MCP_PRIVACY}</p>
      <p>
        The planner itself never uploads your floor plans: they stay in your browser. A plan reaches
        the MCP server only when you ask an AI assistant to use it, and only the plan the assistant
        sends. Your assistant's own privacy terms cover the chat itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits">
      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Free, with no account. Each address can make about 120 requests a minute.</li>
        <li>Up to 50 rooms and 500 pieces of furniture per plan, and 256 KB per request.</li>
        <li>
          The server makes no images; apps that support MCP Apps draw the plan in the chat, and the
          link always shows it in the planner.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For developers: the server is stateless and speaks both current MCP protocol versions. Its
        server card is at{' '}
        <a href={abs(`${MCP_PATH}/server-card`)} class="text-indigo-700 underline">
          /{MCP_PATH}/server-card
        </a>
        , and the source is on{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/EricAndrechek/Room-Planner" class="text-indigo-700 underline">
          GitHub
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <p class="mt-10">
    New to the planner? The{' '}
    <a href={withBase('help/')} class="text-indigo-700 underline">
      help page
    </a>{' '}
    explains every feature, and the{' '}
    <a href={withBase('guides/')} class="text-indigo-700 underline">
      room layout guides
    </a>{' '}
    list furniture sizes and clearances.
  </p>
</Content>
